const e = practitionerB4.find(p => p.slug === 'rag-retrieval-before-answer-quality')!;
---
<InsightArticle title={e.title} description={e.description} date="2026-11-14" type={e.type as 'Analysis' | 'Explainer'} readTime="5 min" standfirst="In our worked example, a document assistant answers 68 of 100 questions correctly. Twenty failures begin before the language model sees the evidence. A single answer score conceals that distinction.">

<p>A document assistant gives a wrong answer. You could try a larger model, rewrite the question or retrieve more text. Start by looking at what the model received. If the supporting passage never reached the prompt, an answer-quality score alone will not tell you which change to make.</p>
<p>Anthropic's <a href={e.sources[0].url}>retrieval explanation</a>, read on 14 November 2026, describes a pipeline that finds document chunks and supplies them to the answering model. That gives us two things to check separately: whether the right material arrived, and whether the answer used it correctly. The numbers below illustrate that distinction; they are not Anthropic's results.</p>
<h2>Mark the supporting passage before testing</h2>
<p>Choose questions whose answers you can establish from the collection. For each one, save the source version and the passage needed to answer it. Where a question needs several passages, write down that requirement. Finding one half of a comparison should not count as complete evidence.</p>
<p>Our fictional test contains 100 answerable questions. Evidence reaches the final prompt for 80. Of those, 68 receive an answer that passes review. We define acceptance to require support from the supplied material, so an unsupported lucky guess would not pass.</p>
<WorkedTable headers={["Gate in the invented run","Passing questions","Share of all questions"]} rows={examples['rag-retrieval-before-answer-quality']} caption="Synthetic nested outcomes for 100 answerable questions. Retrieved means the required supporting passage is present in the final generation prompt." />
<p>Retrieval coverage is 80%. Overall acceptance is 68%. Among the cases with evidence available, acceptance is 85%. Keep all three denominators visible. Reporting only the last figure would conceal the missing evidence on a fifth of the original questions.</p>
<h2>Inspect the final prompt, not only the search result</h2>
<p>A passage can appear in a search result and disappear during reranking, deduplication or prompt assembly. Define the retrieval gate at the point that matters to the answer: the material actually supplied for generation. Retain intermediate results privately when you need to locate where the passage was lost.</p>
<p>For a failed case, first ask whether the document was in the indexed collection. Then inspect the candidates, the selected passages and the assembled prompt. These checks distinguish an ingestion gap from a search failure or an over-tight context budget. They are diagnostic steps we suggest, not measured causes in a real deployment.</p>
<p>Use source identifiers that survive a rebuild. A chunk's position in an array can change when another file is added. Keep a document version and a location within that version so that a reviewer can find the evidence again.</p>
<h2>Change one part and keep the old cases</h2>
<p>If evidence is missing, compare retrieval settings on the same questions before changing the answering model. If the evidence is present but the answer fails, inspect how the question and passages are presented. Save the complete configuration for each run. Otherwise an improvement cannot be attributed to the change you intended to test.</p>
<p>Include exact identifiers as well as ordinary-language questions when your collection contains them. Anthropic's article discusses combining lexical and embedding retrieval. That is a candidate approach to test, not proof that a hybrid setup improves your collection. Keep any added delay and prompt tokens beside the retrieval result.</p>
<p>Questions that have no answer in the collection need their own category. They test whether the assistant admits the gap. Mixing them into a denominator defined as answerable would make retrieval coverage ambiguous. An empty result can be correct for that category.</p>
<h2>The useful tracker entry</h2>
<p>Record collection version, indexing settings, question-set version, evidence-available count and accepted-answer count. Add the number of questions needing multiple sources and the failures in that group. Keep the test small enough to inspect before increasing its size.</p>
<p>A higher end-to-end score is welcome. A record of where the evidence was lost tells you what to try next, and whether the next model release is relevant to the problem you actually have.</p>
<PractitionerNote evidence={e} />
</InsightArticle>
